          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definitions"} Definitions
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#definition_via_pseudogroups"} Definition via pseudogroups
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#definitions_2"} Definitions
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#isomorphisms_of_manifolds"} Isomorphisms of manifolds
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#definition_via_cartologies"} Definition via cartologies
              %li
                %a{:href => "#generalizations"} Generalizations
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %div{:style => "float:right;margin:0 10px 10px 0;"}
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/ChartsOfAManifold.png", :width => "500"}/
          %p
            A
            %em manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            which looks locally like a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %em
              manifold of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-fold
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifold"} infinite-dimensional manifold
          %p
            What “locally looks like” means depends on what sort of structure we are considering a Cartesian space to embody. At one extreme, we can think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            as merely a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            may be considered as carrying more rigid types of structure, such as
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^k
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+structure"} differential structure
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+structure"} smooth structure
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PL+structure"} piecewise-linear (PL) structure
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+function"} analytic structure
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+manifold"} differentiable manifold
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+manifold"} analytic manifold
            etc. By default these are modeled on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            spaces, but most notions have generalizations to a corresponding notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite+dimensional+manifold"} infinite dimensional manifold
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+Geometry+of+Physics+-+An+Introduction"} Frankel
          %p
            In any case, the type of geometry embodied in a particular flavor of manifold is controlled by a particular
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            or, more generally,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of transformations which preserves whatever geometric features one is interested in; cf. Felix Klein’s
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erlangen+program"} Erlanger Programm
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %p
            Here we will focus on the general notion of a manifold. More concrete examples can be found in individual pages such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+manifold"} topological manifold
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
          %p
            We will present two possible definitions. The first, via
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudogroups"} pseudogroups
          %ol
            %li There is in general no notion of morphisms between manifolds. At best, we can only talk about isomorphisms of manifolds.
            %li
              We can only define smooth
              = succeed "-manifolds" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              separately, but not smooth manifolds in general (and the same applies to complex manifolds etc.)
          %p
            The second definition via cartologies was proposed by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
            to solve the above two problems.
          %h3#definition_via_pseudogroups Definition via pseudogroups
          %p
            The setting is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            together with a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudogroup"} pseudogroup
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = \mathbb{R}^n
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphisms"} diffeomorphisms
            between open subsets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %h4#definitions_2 Definitions
          #Chart.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chart"} chart
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              together with an open embedding
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\phi: U \to X\,.
            %p
              Two charts
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: U \to X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi: V \to X
              are
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %strong compatible
              if
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ∘
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi \circ \phi^{-1}: \phi(U \cap V) \to \psi(U \cap V)
            %p
              belongs to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          #Atlas.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %strong atlas
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a family of
              = succeed "-compatible" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi α
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi_\alpha: U_\alpha \to X)_\alpha
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Chart"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi α
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (U_\alpha)_\alpha
              covers
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mi β
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi β
                    %mo ∘
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{\alpha \beta} = \phi_\beta \circ \phi_{\alpha}^{-1}
              are called
              %strong transition functions
              between the charts of the atlas.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              A
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %strong manifold
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              equipped with a
              = succeed "-atlas" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Atlas"}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              This means that we can think of a
              = succeed "-manifold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              %em locally modeled
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              according to the geometry
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              It is almost invariably the case in classical manifold theory that one requires some technical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nice+topological+space"} niceness
              properties on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              underlying a manifold.
            %p Usually, in the definition of manifold it is understood that the underlying topological space
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  is a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+space"} Hausdorff topological space
                  (if not one usually speaks explicitly of a
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-Hausdorff+manifold"} non-Hausdorff manifold
              %li
                %p
                  is a
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracompact+topological+space"} paracompact topological space
            %p
              Often it is also assumed that the topology has a countable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis+of+a+topology"} basis
              as well.
            %p
              In many of the typical cases, this will mean that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metric+space"} metrizable
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theory"} cobordism theory
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              An atlas is
              %em not
              considered an essential part of the structure of a manifold: two different atlases may yield the same manifold structure. This is encoded by the following definition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#IsomorphismOfManifolds"}
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              between manifolds.
          %h4#examples Examples
          %p
            If the term “manifold” appears without further qualification, what is usually meant is a
            %strong
              smooth
              = succeed "-manifold" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} natural number
            %strong dimension
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-manifold" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is the pseudogroup of invertible
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{\infty}
            maps between open sets of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
